  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="3abdbf20-ab91-496c-b655-7db775d06026" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0e64d705-1a60-4342-b809-5c7af161812b" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0">
    <problem>
      <para> Here is a graph of a quadratic function \(f(x)\). What is the minimum value of \(f(x)\)?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b5a76008e28ba16cd2a4d822e995136c0986c341"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> Enter the minimum value of \(f(x)\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The minimum value of \(f(x)\) is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.11.2 and 9.11.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9b01f8bf-8316-425b-8486-d01357b4d5f6" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value." data-solution-options="[&quot;The constant is always the minimum value.&quot;, &quot;The expression \\((x+1)^2\\) is a squared expression, so its value will always be positive or zero.&quot;, &quot;We cannot tell from the expression; we have to graph it.&quot;, &quot;The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The graph that represents \(f(x)=(x+1)^2−4\) has its vertex at \((-1,-4)\).</para>
      <para>Explain how we can tell from the expression \((x+1)^2−4\) that -4 is the minimum value of \(f\) rather than the maximum value.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The coefficient of the quadratic term is positive; therefore, the vertex will be a minimum value.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.11.2 and 9.11.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1e493ddb-22a6-46bd-b07e-12c4b0e1e2a7" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="5">
    <problem>
      <para>\(g(x)=(x−5)^2+6\) defines a quadratic function. Find the vertex of the graph of the function. Then, state whether the vertex corresponds to the maximum or the minimum value of the function.</para>
      <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para> Enter the \(x\)-coordinate. </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is 5.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.11.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f3836141-4bbe-4f2f-8754-8b0e2d7298aa" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="6">
